WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:08.430
In my video on the circle division problem,
I referenced Euler’s Characteristic Formula,

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:12.139
and here I would like to share a particularly
nice proof of this fact.

00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:16.660
It’s very different from the inductive proof
typically given, but I’m not trying to argue

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.300
that this is somehow better or easier to understand
than other proofs.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.320
Instead, I chose this topic to illustrate
one example of the incredible notion of duality,

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.380
and how it can produce wonderfully elegant
math.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.829
First, let’s go over what the theorem states.

00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:36.620
If you draw some dots with some lines between
them, that is, a graph, and if none of the

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:42.030
lines intersect, which is to say you have
a planar graph, and if your drawing is connected,

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:47.359
then Euler’s Formula states that the number
of dots, minus the number of lines, plus the

00:00:47.359 --> 00:00:52.679
number of regions these lines cut the plane
into, including that outer region, will always

00:00:52.679 --> 00:00:54.620
be 2.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.079
Because Euler was originally talking about
3d polyhedra when he found this formula, which

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:03.989
was only later reframed in terms of planar
graphs, instead of saying “dots”, we say

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:07.440
“vertices”, instead of saying “lines”
we say “edges”, and instead of saying

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:14.800
“regions” we say “faces”, hence we
write Euler’s discovery as V-E+F=2.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.300
Before describing the proof, I need to go
through three pieces of graph theory terminology:

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.770
Cycles, spanning trees, and dual graphs.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.530
If you are already familiar with some of these
topics and don’t care to see how I describe

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.040
them, feel free to click the appropriate annotation
to skip ahead.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.380
Imagine a tiny creature is sitting on one
of the vertices.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.420
Let’s name him “Randolph”.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.350
If we think of edges as something Randolph
might travel along, from one vertex to the

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.540
next, we can sensibly talk about a “path”
as being a sequence of edges that Randolph

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:49.310
could travel along, where we don’t allow
him to back-track on the same edge.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.140
A “cycle” is simply a path that ends on
the same vertex where it begins.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:57.760
You might be able to guess how cycles will
be important for our purposes, since each

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.200
they always enclose a set of faces.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.530
Now imagine Randolph wants access to all other
vertices, but that edges are expensive, so

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:11.640
he’ll only buy access to an edge if it gives
him a path to an untouched vertex.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.390
This frugality will leave him with a set of
edges without any cycles, since the edge finishing

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:20.870
off a cycle would always be unnecessary.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:26.160
In general a connected graph without cycles
is called a tree, so named because we can

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.800
move things around to make it look like a
system of branches, and any tree inside a

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.750
graph which touches all vertices is called
a “spanning tree”.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:40.090
Before defining the “dual graph”, which
runs the risk of being confusing, it’s important

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.330
to remember why people actually care about
graphs in the first place.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:48.220
I was actually lying earlier when I said a
graph is a set of dots and lines, really it’s

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.910
a set of anything with any notion of connection,
but we typically represent those things with

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.470
dots and those connections with lines.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:01.090
For instance, Facebook stores an enormous
graph, where vertices are accounts and edges

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:02.480
are friendships.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.819
Although we can use drawings to represent
this graph, the graph itself is the abstract

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:10.890
set of accounts and friendships, distinct
from the drawing.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:16.319
All sorts of things are undrawn graphs: The
set of english words, considered connected

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:21.040
when they differ by one letter; mathematicians,
considered connected if they’ve written

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.020
a paper together; neurons connected by synapses.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:30.480
Or maybe, for those of us reasoning about
the actual drawing of a graph on the plane,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:36.340
we can take the set of faces this graph cuts
the plane into, and consider two of them connected

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.640
if they share an edge.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.200
In other words, if you can draw a graph in
the plane without intersecting the edges,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:49.240
you automatically get a second, as of yet
undrawn graph, whose vertices are faces, and

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.750
whose edges are...well...edges of the original
graph.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:57.190
We call this the “dual” of the original
graph.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:02.430
If you want to represent the dual graph with
dots and lines, first put a dot inside each

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:03.430
of the faces.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:09.300
I personally like to visualize the dot for
the outer region as being a point at infinity,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.099
where you can travel in any direction to get
there.

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:16.880
Next, connect these new dots with new lines
that pass through the centers of each old

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:22.169
line, where lines connected to the point at
infinity can go off the screen in any direction,

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:26.110
as long as it is understood that they all
meet at the same one point.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:30.520
But keep in mind that this is just a drawing
of the dual graph, just as a representation

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:35.210
of facebook accounts and friendships with
dots and lines is just drawing of the social

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:40.180
graph, the dual graph itself is the collection
of faces and edges.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:44.410
The reason I stress this point is to emphasize
that edges of the original graph and edges

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:48.059
of the dual graph are not just related, they
are the same thing!

00:04:48.059 --> 00:04:49.639
(Or at least, I would argue they should be
thought of that way).

00:04:49.639 --> 00:04:54.060
You see, what make the dual graph all kinds
of awesome is the many ways it relates to

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.669
the original graph.

00:04:55.669 --> 00:05:00.229
For example, cycles in the original graph
correspond to connected components of the

00:05:00.229 --> 00:05:05.610
dual graph, and likewise cycles in the dual
graph correspond with connected components

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.289
of the original graph.

00:05:08.289 --> 00:05:09.699
Now for the cool part.

00:05:09.699 --> 00:05:14.889
Suppose our friend Randolph has an alter-ego,
Mortimer, living in the dual graph, traveling

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:20.169
from face to face instead of from vertex to
vertex, passing over edges to do so.

00:05:20.169 --> 00:05:25.180
Let’s say Randolph has bought all the edges
of a spanning tree, and that Mortimer is forbidden

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:27.360
from crossing those edges.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:33.469
It turns out the edges Mortimer has available
to him are guaranteed to form a spanning tree

00:05:33.469 --> 00:05:36.660
of the dual graph!

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.861
To see why, we only need to check the two
defining properties of spanning trees: They

00:05:41.861 --> 00:05:49.039
must give Mortimer access to all faces, and
there can be no cycles.

00:05:49.039 --> 00:05:53.860
The reason he still has access to all faces
is that it would take a cycle in Randolph’s

00:05:53.860 --> 00:06:01.009
spanning tree to insulate him from a face,
but trees cannot have cycles.

00:06:01.009 --> 00:06:06.059
The reason Mortimer cannot traverse a cycle
in the dual graph feel completely symmetric:

00:06:06.059 --> 00:06:10.949
If he could, he would separate one set of
Randolph’s vertices from the rest, so the

00:06:10.949 --> 00:06:18.469
spanning tree from which he is banned could
not have spanned the whole graph.

00:06:18.469 --> 00:06:24.039
So not only does the planar graph have a dual
graph, any spanning tree within that graph

00:06:24.039 --> 00:06:26.879
always has a dual spanning tree in the dual
graph.

00:06:26.879 --> 00:06:33.720
So here’s the kicker: the number of vertices
in a tree is always one more than the number

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.610
edges.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:41.039
To see this, note that after you start with
the root vertex, each new edge gives exactly

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:42.990
1 new vertex.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:47.419
Alternatively, within our narrative, you could
think of Randolph as starting with one vertex,

00:06:47.419 --> 00:06:52.689
and gaining exactly one more for each edge
that he buys in what will become a spanning

00:06:52.689 --> 00:06:53.899
tree.

00:06:53.899 --> 00:06:58.529
Since this tree covers all vertices of our
graph, the number of vertices is one more

00:06:58.529 --> 00:07:01.159
than the number of edges owned by Randolph.

00:07:01.159 --> 00:07:06.490
Moreover, since the remaining edges make up
a spanning tree for Mortimer’s dual graph,

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:10.689
the number of edges he gets is one more than
the number of vertices of the dual graph,

00:07:10.689 --> 00:07:13.419
which are the faces cut out by the original
graph.

00:07:13.419 --> 00:07:17.539
Putting this together, it means the total
number of edges is 2 more than the number

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:22.150
of vertices plus the number of faces, which
is exactly what Euler’s formula states!

